+### Renaming one, or changing a field
+
+Deleting and re-registering was for a long time the only way to change anything, which
+made an ordinary edit (`max_ctx` was wrong, the model is long-context after all) a
+destructive operation. Two subcommands cover the rest, and both change **only** the
+entry in `registry.local.json`:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/catalog.sh update --slug my-llamacpp/<your-model> --set max_ctx=32768 \
+                               --set workload=long-ctx-single    # repeat --set as needed
+bash scripts/catalog.sh rename --slug my-llamacpp/<your-model> --to vllm/<your-model>
+bash scripts/catalog.sh update --slug my-llamacpp/<your-model> --set status=experimental
+```
+
+Values are read as JSON, so `max_ctx=32768` is a number and `moe_cache=true` a bool;
+`status` must be one of the status words in the enum. `--dry-run` prints the plan and
+writes nothing. What they will not do:
+
+* **touch a curated slug** — neither the source nor the target of a rename may be one;
+* **edit provenance** — `origin` is stamped `local` by the loader, and `--set origin=…`
+  is refused (publishing later is §5, which flips that field in a PR);
+* **move your files** — a rename changes the slug, not the model id, so `models.d/`,
+  `composes/` and `compose_path` keep their names. The slug tail is a label; the model
+  id is the file key, and `--set model=…` is refused for exactly that reason;
+* **invent an engine** — the new namespace of a rename must be an engine the catalog
+  knows, yours included (a `profiles-local/engines.d/` profile counts).
+
+The engine of a local entry **is** its slug namespace, so a rename rewrites `engine`
+along with the key and `--set engine=…` is refused — rename to change engines. The layer
+is re-loaded through `load_local_registry()` before either command exits 0; if the edit
+turned it unloadable, the write is rolled back.
